    {
      "id": "61210c12b971aa0fa7ee30e47cfc36cdcc26c8fd",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 2428050108,
          "type": null,
          "body": "This is Yet Another protocol whose developer/standardizer only allows members to view the specification (but somehow managed to allow an open-source dissector for the protocol to escape the cage).\n\nI don't work for an ASAM member, and thus can't see the CMP spec, so:\n\n1. Does this encapsulate the captured protocols in a fashion that would allow the dissector to share code with existing Wireshark dissectors for those protocols (CAN, LIN, FlexRay, ...)?\n2. Is \"LIN encodes \"go to sleep\" frames by using ID 0x3C and setting first byte to 0x00\" something specific to this encapsulation of LIN, or is this something that would belong in the LIN dissector?\n3. From [the description of the protocol](https://www.asam.net/standards/detail/cmp/), this sounds as if either an extcap or a libpcap remote capture module (once I get around to supporting general \"capture over the network\" modules in libpcap) would be useful, allowing Wireshark (or, in the latter case, any *other* libpcap-based program) to capture traffic from a probe.",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2025-04-01T08:18:32.087Z",
          "updated_at": "2025-04-01T08:18:32.087Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 373415532,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": false,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 19437,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "7e9d85ca23b97a3d5374966905ac86d5d848d2fc",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 2429206837,
          "type": null,
          "body": "Hi @guyharris,\n\nI fully share your frustration with paywalled standards but except IETF it seems most are doing this to a certain extend these days.\n\nregarding 1), yes.\n\nregarding 2), this in the LIN specification. If you google for it, you might find for example https://www.lin-cia.org/fileadmin/microsites/lin-cia.org/resources/documents/LIN-Spec_Pac2_1.pdf\n\nTwo important parts of the spec:\n![image](/uploads/7958cf0ae99ff709c7c04b1ecbcde026/image.png){width=795 height=303}\n\n![image](/uploads/6eb26d8472f818365ceb30b4ee3ea749/image.png){width=789 height=430}\n\n\nregarding 3), that is an interesting idea. So far we have captured in pcapng with CMP frames and then converted to pcapng without CMP frames.",
          "author": {
            "id": 6878858,
            "username": "LarsVoelker",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Dr. Lars Völker",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/cccf2084a0438673e9e09f07ed0a5035f6e8815d3d5d888db0023a757c8dce8c?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/LarsVoelker"
          },
          "created_at": "2025-04-01T17:22:30.208Z",
          "updated_at": "2025-04-01T17:22:30.208Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 373415532,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": false,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 19437,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "73f0d7ab7cbcd85b842e408ca7b406d264066649",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 2429358405,
          "type": null,
          "body": "> I fully share your frustration with paywalled standards but except IETF it seems most are doing this to a certain extend these days.\n\nThere's paywalled, which isn't ideal but which, if the price isn't too high, can be handled; there's accountwalled, which, *if* they allow anybody to sign up for an account, doesn't get in the way; and there's do-you-work-for-a-member-of-the-organizationwalled, which is a 5m-thick wall made of solid steel.\n\nThe ITU-T's standards are also not paywalled any more.  The ISO, etc., not so much, but they're mostly just paywalls.\n\n> regarding 1), yes.\n\nOK, for TECMP, I'll look at that.  For ASAM CMP, I'd have to leave that to an ASAM member.  It might involve splitting LIN/CAN/FlexRay dissectors into dissectors that handle the metadata headers and dissectors that handle the payload.",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2025-04-01T19:14:09.423Z",
          "updated_at": "2025-04-01T19:14:09.423Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 373415532,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": false,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 19437,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "175b64a4afd506906ff9b0ccab4b83549fc659ac",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 2430027484,
          "type": null,
          "body": "@guyharris, my understanding is that ASAM is also selling the standards to non-members. If you want to be part of the standardization effort and/or want to download for free, you need a membership. But you can just pay for the PDF, I think. I am trying to figure that out.\n\nAs I have access to the standard, I might be able to answer a question, if you have one.\n\nI find ISO very frustrating as the standards are split often in a lot of small documents. While each document does not cost much, it adds up quite quickly. And paying USD 100 for 16 pages seems also a bit expensive. Then you have to pay per person using the standard. The worst thing about ISO is that you have to pay for writing the standard and after that pay again for getting it. :disappointed:",
          "author": {
            "id": 6878858,
            "username": "LarsVoelker",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Dr. Lars Völker",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/cccf2084a0438673e9e09f07ed0a5035f6e8815d3d5d888db0023a757c8dce8c?s=80&d=identicon",
